    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d195">

                <pb facs="0346" n="314" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v06/pg314"/>

                <head>195. Telegram From the Embassy in Ecuador to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d195-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-12-29T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 29,
                                1958—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>203. My telegram 200.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d195-f2"/> Tobar received Batista’s
                    reply this morning. Batista expresses thanks and suggests Tobar confer with
                    Panamanian and Cuban Ambassadors Washington. Tobar is telegraphing appropriate
                    instructions to Chiriboga. Believes you should have representative present
                    informal discussions. Thinks that these discussions might produce decision 3 or
                    4 friendly countries send covert emissaries confer with Batista and Castro. Some
                    of latter’s agents in United States have been trying establish contact with
                    Tobar here Ecuador. He hitherto has ignored overtures. Now however feels free to
                        <pb facs="0347" n="315" xml:id="pg315"/> make contact. Is anxious to receive
                    whatever suggestions you may offer. Desires be kept up to date and said he would
                    promptly pass to US news of all developments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Ravndal</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d195-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d195-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 737.00/12–2958. Confidential;
                    Priority.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d195-f2" n="2" xml:id="d195-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 200, December 24, in response to a Department of State inquiry,
                    indicated that Tobar was still awaiting Batista’s reply. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>., 737.00/12–2458) In telegram 135 to Quito,
                    December 24, the Department had inquired about the present status of the Tobar
                    initiative in view of its “active study” of the Cuban situation and the
                    “possibility of other initiatives.” (<hi rendition="#italic">ibid</hi>.,
                    737.00/12–1558)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
